WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Small vessels leak a little all along their length, and plasma protein only slows

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.880
the leak. Albumin supplies most of that pull.

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.600
Yet adults born with almost none usually swell only mildly.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.000
What protects them?

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:20.967
Start with where the leak goes.

6
00:00:22.387 --> 00:00:24.947
Filtered fluid enters blind-ended lymph vessels.

7
00:00:26.347 --> 00:00:31.047
Their overlapping cells leave flap-like openings between them, which let fluid in,

8
00:00:31.427 --> 00:00:32.427
not out.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.800
Bigger lymph vessels have valves.

10
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.360
Each segment between two valves, a lymphangion, contracts as a pump.

11
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.820
Squeezing from nearby muscle and from breathing also pushes lymph along.

12
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.280
Recent reviews put an adult's daily filtration near eight liters.

13
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.540
Roughly half is thought to rejoin the blood in lymph nodes.

14
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.260
The rest enters the veins at the base of the neck.

15
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:01.803
Here is the one idea.

16
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:06.823
Edema, swelling of the tissue, is filtration outrunning lymph.

17
00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:12.303
Fluid collects only when more leaves the capillaries than lymph carries away.

18
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:15.107
So there are four ways to swell.

19
00:01:16.547 --> 00:01:20.047
Raise capillary pressure, as in heart failure, and the push grows.

20
00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:24.027
Lower plasma protein, and the hold-back shrinks.

21
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:30.250
Make the small vessels leaky, as in inflammation, and protein escapes, so the

22
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.370
hold-back shrinks too.

23
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.790
Or block the lymph, as in lymphedema, and drainage fails.

24
00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:41.903
Now suppose filtration outruns lymph, and fluid starts to collect in the tissue.

25
00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:46.663
Which way does tissue pressure move, and what does that do to the push?

26
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.430
Tissue pressure rises, so the push shrinks.

27
00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.010
That is one safety factor.

28
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.950
Faster filtration also rinses protein out of the tissue, which strengthens the

29
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.630
hold-back. The third: lymph flow itself rises.

30
00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:12.893
Back to albumin. Take a round teaching example.

31
00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:19.613
Halving albumin lifts the net outward pressure from 1 mmHg to nearly 12.

32
00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:22.873
Washout brings it down to about 8.

33
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:25.383
Lower, but still large.

34
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:30.163
So low protein is not harmless: filtration does rise.

35
00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:35.263
What holds the line is all three safety factors, acting together.

36
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:41.047
In people born without albumin, globulins may partly stand in for it.

37
00:02:42.147 --> 00:02:46.687
That may be why their swelling is common but usually mild, and a few have none.

38
00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.930
So a steady leak is normal, and lymph carries it home.

39
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.810
Swelling begins only when it beats the drain, and the tissue pushes back on three

40
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.150
fronts.
